      "title": "4.4 Power",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "e2c5c034-041b-5340-9823-5e3c8c88a07c",
          "pdf_from": 79,
          "pdf_to": 80,
          "note": "Topic 4.4 with Example 4.2 and the two side boxes (PDF 79-80, printed 76-77). Numerical 4.1's printed answer (9800 W) corresponds to 6 bullets per second rather than per minute; recorded as a finding."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In the definition of work, it is not clear, whether the same amount of work is done in one second or in one hour. The rate, at which work is done, is often of interest in practical applications."
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "definition",
            "title": "Power",
            "text": "Power is the measure of the rate at which work is being done."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If work ΔW is done in a time interval Δt, then the average power P_av during the interval Δt is defined as:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "P_{av} = \\frac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta t} \\tag{4.6}",
            "text_alt": "P_av = ΔW / Δt   (4.6)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If work is expressed as a function of time, the instantaneous power P at any instant is defined as:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "P = \\lim_{\\Delta t \\to 0} \\frac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta t} \\tag{4.7}",
            "text_alt": "P = Limit (Δt → 0) ΔW / Δt   (4.7)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "where ΔW is the work done in short interval of time Δt."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Since}\\quad \\Delta W = \\vec{F}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{d}",
            "text_alt": "Since   ΔW = F · Δd"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Hence}\\quad P = \\frac{\\vec{F}\\cdot\\Delta\\vec{d}}{\\Delta t} = \\vec{F}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta\\vec{d}}{\\Delta t}",
            "text_alt": "Hence   P = F · Δd / Δt = F · (Δd / Δt)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Since}\\quad \\frac{\\Delta\\vec{d}}{\\Delta t} = \\vec{v}",
            "text_alt": "Since   Δd / Δt = v"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Hence}\\quad P = \\vec{F}\\cdot\\vec{v} \\tag{4.8}",
            "text_alt": "Hence   P = F · v   (4.8)"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "definition",
            "title": "Watt",
            "text": "The SI unit of power is watt, defined as one joule of work done in one second."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Sometimes, for example, in the electrical measurements, the unit of work is expressed as watt second. However, a commercial unit of electrical energy is kilowatt-hour."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "One kilowatt- hour is the work done in one hour by an agency whose power is one kilowatt."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Therefore}\\quad 1\\ \\text{kW h} = 1000\\ \\text{W} \\times 3600\\ \\text{s}",
            "text_alt": "Therefore   1 kW h = 1000 W × 3600 s"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{or}\\quad 1\\ \\text{kW h} = 3.6 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\text{J} = 3.6\\ \\text{MJ}",
            "text_alt": "or   1 kW h = 3.6 × 10⁶ J = 3.6 MJ"
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "For Your Information: Approximate Powers",
            "header": [
              "Device",
              "Power (W)"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Jumbo Jet Aircraft",
                "1.3 × 10⁸"
              ],
              [
                "Car at 90 km h⁻¹",
                "1.1 × 10⁵"
              ],
              [
                "Electric heater",
                "2 × 10³"
              ],
              [
                "Coloured TV",
                "120"
              ],
              [
                "Flash light (two cells)",
                "1.5"
              ],
              [
                "Pocket calculator",
                "7.5 × 10⁻⁴"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "example",
            "title": "Example 4.2",
            "problem": "A 70 kg man runs up a long flight of stairs in 4.0 s. The vertical height of the stairs is 4.5 m. Calculate his power output in watts.",
            "steps": [
              "Work done = mgh",
              "Power = mgh / t",
              "P = (70 kg × 9.8 m s⁻² × 4.5 m) / 4 s"
            ],
            "answer": "P = 7.7 × 10² kg m² s⁻³ = 7.7 × 10² W"
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "Do You Know?",
            "text": "It takes about 9 × 10⁹ J of energy to make a car and the car then uses about 1 × 10¹² J of energy from petrol in its life time."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
